 class=3D"whitespace-pre-wrap">Types of Systems

Systems can be broadly classified into Open Systems and Closed Systems base=
d on how they interact with their environment, especially in terms of data =
exchange.

1. Open System

An open system is one that interacts freely with its environment. It shares=
 data, communicates, and can connect with other systems.

=F0=9F=9F=A2 Key Characteristics:

Allows data exchange with external systems

Uses standard protocols and interfaces (e.g., TCP/IP, HTTP)

Interoperable =E2=80=93 can work with other systems

Flexible and expandable

=F0=9F=93=A6 Real-Life Analogy:

Imagine a restaurant with large windows and open doors. People can come in,=
 go out, place orders, and interact freely. It=E2=80=99s accessible and wel=
coming.

=F0=9F=92=BB In Computing:

The Internet is an open system.

Windows, Linux, macOS are open in terms of network communication.

Websites, email services, and APIs are built on open system principles.

2. Closed System

A closed system is one that is isolated from its environment. It does not i=
nteract or share data with other systems easily.

=F0=9F=94=B4 Key Characteristics:

Self-contained and isolated

Limited or no communication with external systems

Often uses proprietary protocols or no network access

Harder to integrate with other systems

=F0=9F=93=A6 Real-Life Analogy:

Think of a locked vault in a bank. Only authorized people with keys can acc=
ess it. It=E2=80=99s secure, but isolated and difficult to interact with.

=F0=9F=92=BB In Computing:

An old machine running software that can't connect to the Internet

A military network with no external access (air-gapped)

Some proprietary embedded systems (e.g., early industrial control units)


