a-multiline=3D""><div class=3D"whitespace-pre-wrap">Types of Networks

Networks are classified based on the area they cover and the purpose they s=
erve. Let=E2=80=99s go through each one:

1. LAN =E2=80=93 Local Area Network
=F0=9F=A7=A0 Definition:

A LAN connects computers and devices within a small geographic area, such a=
s a home, office, school, or lab.

=F0=9F=93=8D Range: Typically within a building or a campus
=F0=9F=9F=A2 Key Features:

High speed (100 Mbps to 10 Gbps)

Privately owned

Low cost and easy to set up

=F0=9F=93=A6 Real-Life Analogy:

Think of a classroom where students pass notes to each other. It=E2=80=99s =
a local, controlled group sharing information quickly.

=F0=9F=9B=A0=EF=B8=8F Example Uses:

File and printer sharing in offices

School computer labs

Home networks

2. MAN =E2=80=93 Metropolitan Area Network
=F0=9F=A7=A0 Definition:

A MAN connects multiple LANs in a city or a large campus. It=E2=80=99s larg=
er than a LAN but smaller than a WAN.

=F0=9F=93=8D Range: Spans a metropolitan area (5=E2=80=9350 km)
=F0=9F=9F=A1 Key Features:

Usually operated by telecom companies or ISPs

Can interconnect various buildings in a city

Often used by governments or large organizations

=F0=9F=93=A6 Real-Life Analogy:

Imagine multiple branches of a bank in a city connected through high-speed =
links to share real-time data.

=F0=9F=9B=A0=EF=B8=8F Example Uses:

City-wide university campuses

Public transportation networks

Cable TV networks

3. WAN =E2=80=93 Wide Area Network
=F0=9F=A7=A0 Definition:

A WAN spans a large geographical area, often a country or continent. It con=
nects multiple LANs and MANs.

=F0=9F=93=8D Range: Global or national scale
=F0=9F=94=B4 Key Features:

Slower than LANs (but improving)

Often uses leased telephone lines, satellites, fiber optics

Managed by multiple organizations

=F0=9F=93=A6 Real-Life Analogy:

The postal system is like a WAN. Letters can be sent across cities, countri=
es, or continents using various systems working together.

=F0=9F=9B=A0=EF=B8=8F Example Uses:

The Internet itself is the world=E2=80=99s largest WAN

Multinational companies connecting branch offices

Global cloud services

4. PAN =E2=80=93 Personal Area Network
=F0=9F=A7=A0 Definition:

A PAN is a small network used by one person, typically covering a few meter=
s.

=F0=9F=93=8D Range: Within 10 meters
=F0=9F=9F=A2 Key Features:

Very short-range

Often uses Bluetooth, Infrared, or USB

Used for connecting personal devices

=F0=9F=93=A6 Real-Life Analogy:

Think of your body as the network=E2=80=94your phone is connected to your s=
martwatch, wireless earbuds, and maybe a laptop.

=F0=9F=9B=A0=EF=B8=8F Example Uses:

Transferring files between phone and laptop

Using a Bluetooth headset

Connecting a phone to a car audio system

5. BAN =E2=80=93 Body Area Network
=F0=9F=A7=A0 Definition:

A BAN (or Body Sensor Network) connects wearable or implanted devices on or=
 around the human body to monitor health or behavior.

=F0=9F=93=8D Range: Around the human body (1=E2=80=932 meters)
=F0=9F=9F=A1 Key Features:

Specialized form of PAN

Focused on medical, fitness, and healthcare

Secure, low-power communication

=F0=9F=93=A6 Real-Life Analogy:

Think of a smart fitness band measuring your steps, heart rate, and sending=
 data to your phone or doctor.

=F0=9F=9B=A0=EF=B8=8F Example Uses:

Heart rate monitors

Glucose level monitors

Fitness trackers like Fitbit or Apple Watch


