1. =E2=9C=85 Client-Server Architecture
=F0=9F=A7=A0 Definition:

In a Client-Server architecture, there is a central server that provides re=
sources or services, and multiple clients that request and use those servic=
es.

Client: Requests data or services (e.g., your browser)

Server: Responds to requests and provides data (e.g., Google=E2=80=99s web =
servers)

=F0=9F=93=A6 Real-Life Analogy:

Think of a restaurant:

Customer (Client) asks for food

Chef/Waiter (Server) prepares and delivers it
Each customer doesn=E2=80=99t cook =E2=80=94 they rely on the central kitch=
en.

=F0=9F=9B=A0=EF=B8=8F Example Use Cases:

Logging into a website (your browser is the client, the website server resp=
onds)

Email services like Gmail

File sharing in corporate networks (files stored on a central server)

=E2=9A=99=EF=B8=8F Features:

Centralized control (easy to manage)

Better security and backups

Can handle many clients

Costlier to set up and maintain

If the server goes down, all clients are affected

2. =F0=9F=94=84 Peer-to-Peer (P2P) Architecture
=F0=9F=A7=A0 Definition:

In a Peer-to-Peer architecture, all devices (peers) are equal. Each device =
can act as both client and server, sharing its own resources with others.

=F0=9F=93=A6 Real-Life Analogy:

Think of a group of friends exchanging notes. No one is the boss. Everyone =
can give and take notes from each other.

=F0=9F=9B=A0=EF=B8=8F Example Use Cases:

File sharing apps like BitTorrent

LAN-based games between friends

Sharing folders between two laptops

=E2=9A=99=EF=B8=8F Features:

No central server required

Easy and cheap to set up

Suitable for small or temporary networks

Less secure and harder to manage

Performance depends on each peer's strength


