iline=3D""><div class=3D"whitespace-pre-wrap">=F0=9F=96=A7 Network Devices =
Explained
1. =F0=9F=93=A1 Modem

Full Form: Modulator-Demodulator

=F0=9F=A7=A0 What It Does:

Converts digital signals (used by computers) into analog signals (used by p=
hone lines/cables) =E2=80=94 and vice versa.

Connects your home network to your ISP (Internet Service Provider).

=F0=9F=93=A6 Real-Life Analogy:

A translator between two people speaking different languages =E2=80=94 one =
speaks digital, the other analog.

=F0=9F=94=A7 Example:

When you use BSNL broadband or Airtel fiber, the modem helps you connect to=
 the internet.

2. =F0=9F=94=8C Ethernet Card

Also called Network Interface Card (NIC)

=F0=9F=A7=A0 What It Does:

Allows a device (like a PC) to connect to a wired network using an Ethernet=
 cable.

Every Ethernet card has a unique MAC address.

=F0=9F=93=A6 Real-Life Analogy:

Like a SIM card in a phone =E2=80=94 it=E2=80=99s the identity and connecto=
r to the network.

3. =F0=9F=94=97 RJ45 Connector
=F0=9F=A7=A0 What It Does:

A standard connector used for Ethernet cables.

Looks like a big telephone plug.

Has 8 pins and connects your Ethernet cable to a device.

=F0=9F=93=A6 Real-Life Analogy:

Like the plug of a charger =E2=80=94 without it, you can=E2=80=99t connect =
your device to power (or in this case, network).

4. =F0=9F=94=81 Repeater
=F0=9F=A7=A0 What It Does:

Boosts and retransmits weak or long-distance signals in a network.

Used to extend the range of a network without changing the data.

=F0=9F=93=A6 Real-Life Analogy:

Think of someone shouting your message further down a hallway so the last p=
erson hears it clearly.

=F0=9F=94=A7 Example:

Used to extend Wi-Fi signals in a large house.

5. =F0=9F=93=A4 Hub
=F0=9F=A7=A0 What It Does:

A basic device that connects multiple computers in a LAN.

Broadcasts incoming data to all ports, regardless of the destination.

=F0=9F=93=A6 Real-Life Analogy:

Like a group WhatsApp message =E2=80=94 everyone receives it even if it=E2=
=80=99s meant for just one person.

=F0=9F=94=A7 Limitation:

Not secure or efficient =E2=80=94 replaced mostly by switches today.

6. =F0=9F=94=80 Switch
=F0=9F=A7=A0 What It Does:

Like a hub, but smarter.

Sends data only to the specific device it=E2=80=99s intended for, not all.

=F0=9F=93=A6 Real-Life Analogy:

Like a personalized mailman =E2=80=94 delivers letters only to the correct =
house, not every house.

=F0=9F=94=A7 Example:

Used in offices or homes to connect multiple devices efficiently.

7. =F0=9F=8C=8D Router
=F0=9F=A7=A0 What It Does:

Connects different networks (like your home network to the internet).

Assigns IP addresses, manages traffic, and routes data.

=F0=9F=93=A6 Real-Life Analogy:

Think of it as a traffic controller at a junction, directing vehicles (data=
) to the correct road (device/network).

