line=3D""><div class=3D"whitespace-pre-wrap">=F0=9F=93=9A Layered Architect=
ure (Protocol Layering)
=F0=9F=A7=A0 What Is It?

Layered Architecture is a structured way of designing communication in a ne=
twork by dividing the entire task into smaller, manageable parts, called la=
yers.

Each layer has a specific role and works independently, but communicates wi=
th the layers above and below it.

=E2=9A=99=EF=B8=8F Why Layering?

Simplifies complex communication

Easier to troubleshoot and upgrade

Layers can be modified or replaced without affecting others

Promotes standardization

=F0=9F=93=A6 Real-Life Analogy: Sending a Letter by Mail

Let=E2=80=99s say you want to send a birthday card to a friend in another c=
ity. Here's how this process works in layers:

Layer	Real-Life Step	Responsibility
1	You write the message	Application layer (what you want to say)
2	You put it in an envelope	Formatting and preparing the message
3	You write the address on it	Routing the message
4	You drop it in a mailbox	Sending to the postal network
5	The post office sorts &amp; routes it	Intermediate delivery steps
6	The card reaches your friend	Final delivery

Each step does its own job, but together, they make the whole communication=
 successful. That=E2=80=99s protocol layering in action!

=F0=9F=93=B6 In Networking:
=E2=9C=85 When Communication Is Simple:

A single protocol (like Bluetooth between two phones) might be enough.

=F0=9F=94=84 When Communication Is Complex:

For something like web browsing, data needs to go through multiple stages:

Converting your request

Routing it over the internet

Receiving it from the server

Reconstructing the webpage

=E2=9E=A1=EF=B8=8F So, multiple layers are used, and each layer has its own=
 protocol to handle specific tasks.

=F0=9F=94=A7 Example: Layers in Action (simplified)

When you open a website like www.example.com:

Application Layer =E2=80=93 You type the website address.

Transport Layer =E2=80=93 Your request is broken into smaller chunks (TCP).

Network Layer =E2=80=93 The request is given a destination (IP address).

Data Link Layer =E2=80=93 Converts it into a format that can travel through=
 wires or Wi-Fi.

Physical Layer =E2=80=93 It=E2=80=99s sent as electrical signals or radio w=
aves.

At the receiving end (the web server), these steps happen in reverse.

=F0=9F=A7=A0 Summary:
Concept	Description
Layered Architecture	Dividing network communication into logical layers
Why it=E2=80=99s used	Makes complex tasks manageable, modular, and easier t=
o troubleshoot
Each layer	Has its own protocol and job
Also called	Protocol Layering


