a-multiline=3D""><div class=3D"whitespace-pre-wrap">1. =F0=9F=93=98 OSI Mod=
el (Open Systems Interconnection)
=F0=9F=A7=A0 Definition:

A theoretical framework developed by the ISO (International Organization fo=
r Standardization) to standardize how different network devices communicate=
.

Has 7 layers

Mostly used for understanding and teaching

Helps break down networking into clear, modular parts

=F0=9F=93=9A The 7 Layers (Top to Bottom):
Layer Number	Layer Name	Function
7=EF=B8=8F=E2=83=A3	Application	User interface (e.g., web browsers)
6=EF=B8=8F=E2=83=A3	Presentation	Data translation, encryption, compression
5=EF=B8=8F=E2=83=A3	Session	Managing sessions between applications
4=EF=B8=8F=E2=83=A3	Transport	Reliable delivery, error recovery (TCP)
3=EF=B8=8F=E2=83=A3	Network	Logical addressing and routing (IP)
2=EF=B8=8F=E2=83=A3	Data Link	Physical addressing (MAC), error detection
1=EF=B8=8F=E2=83=A3	Physical	Actual transmission (cables, Wi-Fi, signals)
=F0=9F=93=A6 Real-Life Analogy for OSI:

Sending a package:

You write a letter (Application)

You encrypt it (Presentation)

You start a session with the post office (Session)

You ensure the letter is split into pages and numbered (Transport)

You write the receiver=E2=80=99s address (Network)

You label the envelope (Data Link)

You hand it over to the delivery person (Physical)

2. =F0=9F=A7=91=E2=80=8D=F0=9F=92=BB TCP/IP Model (Transmission Control Pro=
tocol/Internet Protocol)
=F0=9F=A7=A0 Definition:

A practical model developed by the U.S. Department of Defense used to desig=
n the modern internet.

Has 4 layers

Is implemented in real-world networking

Protocols like HTTP, TCP, IP, FTP, etc. work based on this

=F0=9F=93=9A The 4 Layers:
TCP/IP Layer	Equivalent OSI Layer(s)	Function
Application	Application, Presentation, Session	User interface and applicati=
on services
Transport	Transport	End-to-end communication (TCP, UDP)
Internet	Network	IP addressing and routing
Network Access	Data Link + Physical	Physical data transmission

