 Transmits raw bits (0s and 1s), not meaningful data. In short: It moves da=
ta, but doesn=E2=80=99t understand it. =E2=9A=99=EF=B8=8F Key Responsibilit=
ies: Feature Description =F0=9F=94=8C Physical Transmission Moves electrica=
l, optical, or radio signals over a medium (like wires, fiber, or air). =F0=
=9F=93=A1 Medium Type Uses cables, fiber optics, radio waves, etc. =F0=9F=
=93=8A Bit Rate Controls how fast bits are transmitted =E2=80=93 measured i=
n bps (bits per second). =E2=9A=A1 Signal Encoding Defines how a 1 or 0 is =
represented =E2=80=94 maybe a high voltage =3D 1, low voltage =3D 0. =F0=9F=
=9A=AB No Understanding of Data It doesn=E2=80=99t care if it=E2=80=99s a v=
ideo call or a website =E2=80=93 it just transmits bits. =F0=9F=92=A1 Examp=
les of Physical Layer Devices: Device What It Does =F0=9F=A7=B5 Ethernet ca=
bles (LAN) Carry electrical signals between devices. =F0=9F=96=A5=EF=B8=8F =
Network Interface Card (NIC) Converts data from a computer into signals. =
=F0=9F=94=8C Hubs Broadcast incoming signals to all connected devices. =F0=
=9F=94=80 Switches (Layer 1 functionality) In basic form, can repeat signal=
s (though modern switches operate at Layer 2). =F0=9F=94=A6 Fiber Optic Cab=
le Sends data using light pulses. =F0=9F=93=A1 Wi-Fi Antennas Transmit/rece=
ive radio signals as bits. Layer 2 =E2=80=93 The Data Link Layer (OSI Model=
) =F0=9F=93=8C What Is It? Layer 2 in the OSI model =E2=80=94 just above th=
e Physical Layer. Manages communication between two devices on the same net=
work segment (e.g., PC to switch). Converts raw bits from the Physical Laye=
r into structured data frames and ensures error-free, orderly delivery. =F0=
=9F=A7=B1 Key Functions of the Data Link Layer: Function Explanation =F0=9F=
=93=A6 Framing Breaks data into frames, like small parcels. =F0=9F=8F=B7=EF=
=B8=8F MAC Addressing Adds sender and receiver MAC addresses to each frame =
so devices on the LAN know where to send it. =F0=9F=9B=A1=EF=B8=8F Error De=
tection Uses methods like CRC (Cyclic Redundancy Check) to detect corrupted=
 frames. =F0=9F=9A=A6 Flow Control Manages how fast data is sent to avoid o=
verwhelming slower receivers. =F0=9F=94=81 Acknowledgement &amp; Retransmis=
sion (Sometimes) Ensures frames were received and re-sends if errors occur.=
 =F0=9F=91=A5 Node-to-Node Delivery Deals with direct communication between=
 devices on the same physical network (e.g., your laptop =E2=86=94 router).=
 =F0=9F=94=8C Works Only in Local Network: Does not go across the Internet =
or multiple routers. Works on LAN-level communication, not between cities o=
r continents. =F0=9F=94=A7 Examples of Data Link Protocols: Protocol Descri=
ption =F0=9F=94=97 Ethernet Most common LAN technology (used in most wired =
networks). =F0=9F=93=8E PPP (Point-to-Point Protocol) Used for direct conne=
ctions like between two routers. =F0=9F=94=81 HDLC High-level protocol used=
 in serial communication. =F0=9F=A7=A0 Common Layer 2 Devices and Component=
s: Component Function =F0=9F=92=B3 Network Interface Cards (NICs) Every NIC=
 has a unique MAC address burned into it. =F0=9F=94=80 Switches Use MAC add=
resses to forward frames only to the correct port (unlike hubs). =F0=9F=A7=
=B1 Bridges Connect two LAN segments and filter traffic based on MAC addres=
ses. Layer 3 =E2=80=93 The Network Layer (OSI Model) =F0=9F=93=8C What Is I=
t? 3rd Layer in the OSI Model. Responsible for routing data between differe=
nt networks (LAN to LAN, or LAN to the internet). Uses logical (IP) address=
ing to identify where data should go. The data unit at this layer is called=
 a packet. =F0=9F=94=A7 Key Responsibilities: Function Description =F0=9F=
=A7=AD Routing Chooses the best path for the packet to travel between netwo=
rks using routers. =F0=9F=8F=A0 IP Addressing Every device has a logical ad=
dress (IP) =E2=80=94 like a street address for data delivery. =F0=9F=93=A6 =
Packetization Breaks data into packets and puts IP info on them for deliver=
y. =F0=9F=94=81 Reassembly At the destination, reassembles the packets into=
 the original data. =F0=9F=9A=A6 Traffic Control Can manage congestion usin=
g methods like TTL (Time To Live). =F0=9F=94=92 Basic Security Routers can =
filter packets by IP to block or allow traffic. =F0=9F=94=97 Connection Typ=
es Can work in connectionless (e.g., IP) or connection-oriented (e.g., MPLS=
) mode. | Feature | Description | | ------------ | ------------------------=
---------------------- | | Layer Number | 3 | | Data Unit | Packet | | Addr=
essing | Logical (IP address) | | Device | Router | | Scope | Inter-network=
 (LAN to WAN) | | Key Function | Routing packets between networks | | Proto=
cols | IP, ICMP, IPsec | | Analogy | GPS: chooses the best route across man=
y cities | Layer 4 =E2=80=93 The Transport Layer (OSI Model) =F0=9F=93=8C W=
hat Is It? The 4th layer in the OSI model. Handles end-to-end communication=
 =E2=80=94 from one device to another across a network. Ensures that the da=
ta is delivered completely, correctly, and in order. Supports both: Reliabl=
e (connection-oriented) communication: via TCP Unreliable (connectionless) =
communication: via UDP =F0=9F=A7=A0 Main Responsibilities of the Transport =
Layer Function Description =E2=9C=85 Reliability Ensures all data arrives i=
ntact (TCP) =F0=9F=93=A6 Segmentation &amp; Reassembly Splits data into sma=
ll pieces at the sender, and reassembles it at the receiver =E2=8F=B3 Flow =
Control Prevents sending too much data too fast (like not overloading the r=
eceiver) =F0=9F=94=84 Error Detection Checks for errors in data and can req=
uest retransmission =F0=9F=94=97 Connection Establishment Sets up, manages,=
 and tears down a virtual connection (in TCP) =F0=9F=93=B6 Multiplexing All=
ows multiple apps (browser, WhatsApp) to use the network at the same time o=
n one device =F0=9F=A7=AA Common Transport Layer Protocols Protocol Type Us=
ed In TCP (Transmission Control Protocol) Connection-oriented Web browsing =
(HTTP/HTTPS), Email (SMTP), File Transfer (FTP) UDP (User Datagram Protocol=
) Connectionless Video streaming, Online games, VoIP (e.g., Zoom, Skype), D=
NS =F0=9F=93=B6 TCP vs UDP =E2=80=93 A Comparison Feature TCP UDP Connectio=
n Yes (3-way handshake) No Reliable =E2=9C=85 Yes =E2=9D=8C No Ordered Deli=
very =E2=9C=85 Yes =E2=9D=8C No Speed Slower (due to reliability features) =
Faster Use Case Web, email, file transfer Streaming, gaming, DNS Overhead H=
igh Low =F0=9F=A7=A9 Segmentation &amp; Reassembly Large data is split into=
 segments to travel efficiently. Each segment has: A sequence number so the=
y can be reassembled in order. Error-checking data to verify integrity. =F0=
=9F=9B=A1=EF=B8=8F Error Handling TCP can detect: Lost segments Corrupted d=
ata It retransmits lost/corrupt data to ensure complete delivery. =F0=9F=93=
=B1 Multiplexing Explained Suppose you're using: Chrome (port 80/443), What=
sApp (port 5222), Spotify (port 4070) all at once. The Transport Layer uses=
 port numbers to make sure data from each app goes to the right one =E2=80=
=94 like room numbers in a hotel. Layer 5 =E2=80=93 The Session Layer (OSI =
Model) =F0=9F=93=8C What Is It? The 5th layer in the OSI model. Manages ses=
sions between two applications (not just devices). A session is a continuou=
s exchange of information =E2=80=94 like a conversation between two applica=
tions. =F0=9F=A7=A0 Key Responsibilities of the Session Layer Function Desc=
ription =F0=9F=94=97 Establishing Sessions Initiates communication between =
devices or apps (like starting a phone call). =E2=8F=B3 Maintaining Session=
s Keeps the session alive and tracks the exchange of data (like keeping the=
 call connected). =F0=9F=93=B4 Terminating Sessions Gracefully ends the com=
munication once it=E2=80=99s done. =F0=9F=A7=AD Synchronization Adds checkp=
oints (sync points) in case of interruption =E2=80=94 helps resume from whe=
re it stopped. =F0=9F=93=8F Dialog Control Manages who can talk and when (h=
alf-duplex or full-duplex). =F0=9F=93=8B Session Recovery If connection is =
lost, it can help restart from the last sync point. =E2=9C=85 Acknowledgeme=
nt Tracking Confirms how much data has been received so far. ayer 6 =E2=80=
=93 The Presentation Layer (OSI Model) =F0=9F=93=8C What Is It? The 6th lay=
er of the OSI model. Focuses on how data is presented =E2=80=94 its format,=
 structure, encryption, and compression. Think of it as the translator, edi=
tor, and security guard for data before it reaches the application. =F0=9F=
=A7=A0 Main Responsibilities Function Description =F0=9F=9B=A1=EF=B8=8F Enc=
ryption/Decryption Secures data so only the right receiver can understand i=
t (e.g., HTTPS). =F0=9F=93=A6 Compression/Decompression Shrinks data size f=
or faster transmission; expands it at the receiver=E2=80=99s side. =F0=9F=
=8E=A8 Data Formatting Ensures data is structured in a format that the appl=
ication understands (e.g., JSON, XML, JPEG). =F0=9F=8C=90 Translation Conve=
rts data from one system=E2=80=99s format to another (e.g., EBCDIC to ASCII=
). =F0=9F=96=BC=EF=B8=8F Graphic/Media Conversion Converts media files like=
 images, audio, or video to proper formats. =F0=9F=A7=AC Real-Life Analogy:=
 Subtitle Translator in a Movie Imagine watching a movie in French with Eng=
lish subtitles. The movie is the raw data (like binary). The translator add=
s subtitles in your language (formatting/translation). If the movie is in H=
D but compressed to stream faster =E2=80=94 that's compression. If access i=
s password-protected, it=E2=80=99s encryption. That translator is the Prese=
ntation Layer. Layer 7 =E2=80=93 The Application Layer (OSI Model) =F0=9F=
=93=8C What Is It? The 7th (topmost) layer of the OSI Model. It provides a =
user interface to access network services. This is the layer closest to the=
 end user =E2=80=94 it=E2=80=99s what we interact with directly, like web b=
rowsers, email apps, or file-sharing tools. =F0=9F=94=8D It's not the app i=
tself (like Chrome), but the part that allows the app to use the network. =
=F0=9F=8E=AF Main Responsibilities Function Description =F0=9F=93=A4 File T=
ransfer Allows uploading/downloading files (e.g., FTP) =F0=9F=93=A7 Email S=
ervices Sending and receiving email (SMTP, IMAP) =F0=9F=96=A8=EF=B8=8F Netw=
ork Printing Sending print jobs to shared/network printers =F0=9F=A7=91=E2=
=80=8D=F0=9F=92=BB Remote Login Accessing another computer via network (e.g=
., SSH, Telnet) =F0=9F=92=AC Messaging Real-time chat/messaging over networ=
k =F0=9F=8C=8D Web Browsing Loading and navigating websites (HTTP/HTTPS) =
=F0=9F=94=8C Network Services Access Accessing shared services (e.g., file =
servers, DNS lookup) Real-Life Analogy: Imagine you're ordering food using =
a food delivery app like Swiggy or Zomato: You open the app (like a browser=
 or email client). You browse restaurants (HTTP request to a web server). Y=
ou place an order (send data). The app receives updates about your delivery=
 (response from server). The entire interaction happens at the Application =
Layer =E2=80=94 what you see and control directly as a user. The underlying=
 layers (Layers 1=E2=80=936) just support this experience invisibly. =F0=9F=
=A7=A0 What It Doesn=E2=80=99t Do: It doesn't handle data transmission, enc=
ryption, or routing. Those are jobs for lower layers (like Transport or Net=
work layers). Application layer simply uses the services provided by the lo=
wer layers to make communication possible.</div></div></div></div></div><di=
