=3D""><div class=3D"whitespace-pre-wrap">1. Simplex Communication

Definition: Data flows only in one direction =E2=80=94 one device is always=
 the sender, and the other is always the receiver.

Example:

A TV broadcast =E2=80=94 the station sends signals to your TV, but your TV =
never sends data back.

A keyboard =E2=80=94 sends keystrokes to the computer but never receives da=
ta back.

Key point: No return channel exists.

2. Half-Duplex Communication

Definition: Data flows both directions, but only one direction at a time.

Example:

Walkie-talkies =E2=80=94 you can speak or listen, but not both at once. You=
 press the button to talk, release to listen.

Older CB radios.

Key point: The channel is shared, so devices take turns sending and receivi=
ng.

3. Full-Duplex Communication

Definition: Data flows both directions simultaneously =E2=80=94 both device=
s can send and receive at the same time.

Example:

Telephone calls =E2=80=94 you can talk and hear the other person at the sam=
e time.

Modern Ethernet connections.

Key point: Requires separate channels or advanced signaling so sending/rece=
iving don=E2=80=99t interfere.


