3" data-multiline=3D""><div class=3D"whitespace-pre-wrap">. Point-to-Point =
Connection

Definition: A dedicated link exists between exactly two devices.

Key points:

The entire capacity of the link is reserved for these two devices.

No other device can use the connection.

Example:

A direct USB cable between a printer and a computer.

A telephone line between two phones in an old landline system.

2. Multipoint (Multidrop) Connection

Definition: More than two devices share the same physical link.

Key points:

Capacity is shared among all connected devices.

The sharing can happen in two ways:

a) Spatially Shared

Multiple devices can use the link at the same time, but in different physic=
al segments or channels.

Example:

Cable TV network where different channels can be watched simultaneously.

Ethernet hub where separate collision domains are managed.

b) Time-Shared

Devices take turns using the link; only one device transmits at a time.

Example:

Old bus-based computer networks.

Time-Division Multiplexing (TDM) phone lines.

