Bus Topology
Definition

All devices are connected to a single central cable, called the bus or back=
bone.

The bus acts as a shared communication medium =E2=80=94 messages from a dev=
ice travel along the bus in both directions and can be received by all devi=
ces.

Devices tap into the bus through drop lines and taps (connectors).

Number of Links

Only one main backbone cable is required.

Each device is connected to this cable via a drop line =E2=80=94 no dedicat=
ed point-to-point links.

Advantages

Easy installation

Less cabling compared to mesh or star.

Low initial cost

Fewer cables and no central hub required.

Good for small networks

Works well when the number of devices is small and traffic is light.

Disadvantages

Difficult troubleshooting

If the backbone fails, the entire network is down.

Limited cable length and number of devices

More devices or longer cables cause signal degradation.

Low scalability

Adding devices increases chances of collisions and slows performance.

Poor fault isolation

It=E2=80=99s hard to determine where a fault is on the bus.

Performance drops with heavy traffic

Shared medium means more collisions.

Example

Early Ethernet networks (10Base-2, 10Base-5).

Some small office setups in the 1980s=E2=80=931990s.

