where n =3D number of devices in the ring.

Each device requires 2 I/O ports =E2=80=94 one to connect to the device on =
the left and one to the device on the right.

Advantages

Orderly data flow

Data passes through devices one at a time =E2=80=94 no collisions.

Equal access

Every device gets an equal opportunity to transmit (often using a token-pas=
sing method).

Easy fault isolation (in smaller rings)

The point of failure can be located by checking the loop.

Predictable performance

Suitable for networks with predictable, steady traffic.

Disadvantages

Single point of failure

In a unidirectional ring, one cable or device failure can break the whole n=
etwork (unless dual ring or fault-tolerant design is used).

Difficult installation &amp; reconfiguration

Adding/removing devices can temporarily disrupt the network.

Latency increases with devices

Data must pass through multiple intermediate devices.

Example

Old FDDI (Fiber Distributed Data Interface) networks.

Token Ring LANs (IBM standard).

SONET/SDH in telecom backbones.

