lass=3D"whitespace-pre-wrap">Guided Media in Computer Networks

Definition:
Guided media are transmission media that provide a physical path (conduit) =
for signals to travel between devices.
Signals are guided through a solid medium such as copper wires or glass fib=
er.

Types of Guided Media:

1. Twisted-Pair Cable

Structure: Two copper wires twisted around each other.

Purpose of Twisting: Reduces electromagnetic interference (EMI) and crossta=
lk.

Variants:

UTP (Unshielded Twisted Pair) =E2=86=92 cheaper, widely used in LANs (Ether=
net).

STP (Shielded Twisted Pair) =E2=86=92 has a metallic shield for better prot=
ection.

Pros: Cheap, flexible, easy to install.

Cons: Susceptible to noise and attenuation, limited distance and bandwidth.

Use Case: Telephone lines, Ethernet LAN cables (Cat5e, Cat6).

2. Coaxial Cable

Structure: Central copper conductor, surrounded by insulation, metallic shi=
eld, and outer jacket.

Shield Function: Protects against EMI better than twisted pair.

Pros: Higher bandwidth and distance than twisted pair; less interference.

Cons: Thicker, less flexible, more expensive.

Use Case: Cable TV, broadband internet, older Ethernet (10BASE2/10BASE5).

3. Fiber-Optic Cable

Structure: Glass or plastic core that carries light pulses (not electricity=
).

Transmission: Light travels via total internal reflection inside the core.

Pros:

Immune to EMI (light, not electricity).

Very high bandwidth (Gbps to Tbps).

Long distance communication (hundreds of km without signal loss).

More secure (harder to tap).

Cons: Expensive, fragile, requires skilled installation.

Use Case: Internet backbone, long-distance telecom, data centers.

