<ul data-start=3D"3785" data-end=3D"4086">
<li data-start=3D"3785" data-end=3D"4086">
<p data-start=3D"3787" data-end=3D"3815"><strong data-start=3D"3787" data-e=
nd=3D"3813">Least affected Medium:</strong></p>
<ul data-start=3D"3819" data-end=3D"4086">
<li data-start=3D"3819" data-end=3D"4015">
<p data-start=3D"3821" data-end=3D"3844"><strong data-start=3D"3821" data-e=
nd=3D"3842">Fiber Optic Cable</strong></p>
<ul data-start=3D"3850" data-end=3D"4015">
<li data-start=3D"3850" data-end=3D"3902">
<p data-start=3D"3852" data-end=3D"3902">Uses <strong data-start=3D"3857" d=
ata-end=3D"3866">light</strong>, not electricity =E2=86=92 immune to EMI.</=
p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"3908" data-end=3D"3953">
<p data-start=3D"3910" data-end=3D"3953">Very low attenuation over long dis=
tances.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"3959" data-end=3D"4015">
<p data-start=3D"3961" data-end=3D"4015">No distortion (light frequencies r=
emain consistent).</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"4019" data-end=3D"4086">
<p data-start=3D"4021" data-end=3D"4086">Hence, best medium for long-distan=
ce, high-speed communication.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start=3D"4088" data-end=3D"4091">
<h2 data-start=3D"4093" data-end=3D"4108"><strong data-start=3D"4096" data-=
end=3D"4106">Q4 (a)</strong></h2>
<p data-start=3D"4109" data-end=3D"4289"><strong data-start=3D"4109" data-e=
nd=3D"4122">Question:</strong><br data-start=3D"4122" data-end=3D"4125">
Can we convert a star topology comprising of five computers and a hub into =
a dual ring topology? How is network setup cost and scalability affected? J=
ustify. (5M)</p>
<p data-start=3D"4291" data-end=3D"4304"><strong data-start=3D"4291" data-e=
nd=3D"4302">Answer:</strong></p>
<ul data-start=3D"4305" data-end=3D"4882">
<li data-start=3D"4305" data-end=3D"4459">
<p data-start=3D"4307" data-end=3D"4459"><strong data-start=3D"4307" data-e=
nd=3D"4314">Yes</strong>, a star can be reconfigured into a <strong data-st=
art=3D"4350" data-end=3D"4372">dual ring topology</strong> by connecting ea=
ch node in a ring-like manner with two connections (for redundancy).</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"4460" data-end=3D"4634">
<p data-start=3D"4462" data-end=3D"4480"><strong data-start=3D"4462" data-e=
nd=3D"4478">Cost Impact:</strong></p>
<ul data-start=3D"4484" data-end=3D"4634">
<li data-start=3D"4484" data-end=3D"4560">
<p data-start=3D"4486" data-end=3D"4560">Increases cost because additional =
cabling is required to form the rings.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"4564" data-end=3D"4634">
<p data-start=3D"4566" data-end=3D"4634">Star (cheap with hub/switch) =E2=
=86=92 Dual ring (expensive, more wiring).</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"4635" data-end=3D"4882">
<p data-start=3D"4637" data-end=3D"4655"><strong data-start=3D"4637" data-e=
nd=3D"4653">Scalability:</strong></p>
<ul data-start=3D"4659" data-end=3D"4882">
<li data-start=3D"4659" data-end=3D"4750">
<p data-start=3D"4661" data-end=3D"4750">Dual ring is <strong data-start=3D=
"4674" data-end=3D"4691">less scalable</strong> than star because adding/re=
moving nodes breaks the ring.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"4754" data-end=3D"4882">
<p data-start=3D"4756" data-end=3D"4882">Star is <strong data-start=3D"4764=
" data-end=3D"4783">easily scalable</strong> (just plug into hub/switch).<b=
r data-start=3D"4812" data-end=3D"4815">
=F0=9F=91=89 So, conversion is possible but costly and reduces scalability.=
</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start=3D"4884" data-end=3D"4887">
<h2 data-start=3D"4889" data-end=3D"4904"><strong data-start=3D"4892" data-=
end=3D"4902">Q4 (b)</strong></h2>
<p data-start=3D"4905" data-end=3D"5042"><strong data-start=3D"4905" data-e=
nd=3D"4918">Question:</strong><br data-start=3D"4918" data-end=3D"4921">
Illustrate a topology that has only two neighbours for each device. Write t=
he pros and cons of the given topology. (5M)</p>
<p data-start=3D"5044" data-end=3D"5057"><strong data-start=3D"5044" data-e=
nd=3D"5055">Answer:</strong></p>
<ul data-start=3D"5058" data-end=3D"5158">
<li data-start=3D"5058" data-end=3D"5158">
<p data-start=3D"5060" data-end=3D"5090"><strong data-start=3D"5060" data-e=
nd=3D"5088">Topology =3D Ring Topology</strong></p>
<ul data-start=3D"5094" data-end=3D"5158">
<li data-start=3D"5094" data-end=3D"5158">
<p data-start=3D"5096" data-end=3D"5158">Each device connected to exactly 2=
 neighbors (left &amp; right).</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start=3D"5160" data-end=3D"5171"><strong data-start=3D"5160" data-e=
nd=3D"5169">Pros:</strong></p>
<ol data-start=3D"5172" data-end=3D"5339">
<li data-start=3D"5172" data-end=3D"5229">
<p data-start=3D"5175" data-end=3D"5229">Simple &amp; organized (token pass=
ing avoids collision).</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"5230" data-end=3D"5294">
<p data-start=3D"5233" data-end=3D"5294">Equal access for all devices (no u=
nfair bandwidth hogging).</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"5295" data-end=3D"5339">
<p data-start=3D"5298" data-end=3D"5339">Performs better than bus at high l=
oads.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p data-start=3D"5341" data-end=3D"5352"><strong data-start=3D"5341" data-e=
nd=3D"5350">Cons:</strong></p>
<ol data-start=3D"5353" data-end=3D"5492">
<li data-start=3D"5353" data-end=3D"5415">
<p data-start=3D"5356" data-end=3D"5415">Failure of a single node/link can =
disrupt entire network.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"5416" data-end=3D"5450">
<p data-start=3D"5419" data-end=3D"5450">Troubleshooting is difficult.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"5451" data-end=3D"5492">
<p data-start=3D"5454" data-end=3D"5492">Adding/removing devices is not eas=
y.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p data-start=3D"5494" data-end=3D"5571">=F0=9F=91=89 Hence, ring ensures f=
airness but lacks robustness compared to star/mesh.</p>
