<hr data-start=3D"132" data-end=3D"135">
<h2 data-start=3D"137" data-end=3D"307"><strong data-start=3D"140" data-end=
=3D"307">Q1. Discuss the significance of layering in the OSI model for faci=
litating network communication. Explain process-to-process delivery in the =
transport layer. (10M)</strong></h2>
<h3 data-start=3D"309" data-end=3D"324"><strong data-start=3D"313" data-end=
=3D"324">Answer:</strong></h3>
<p data-start=3D"325" data-end=3D"367"><strong data-start=3D"325" data-end=
=3D"367">Significance of Layering in OSI Model:</strong></p>
<ol data-start=3D"368" data-end=3D"914">
<li data-start=3D"368" data-end=3D"451">
<p data-start=3D"371" data-end=3D"451"><strong data-start=3D"371" data-end=
=3D"386">Modularity:</strong> Each layer performs a specific set of functio=
ns independently.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"452" data-end=3D"551">
<p data-start=3D"455" data-end=3D"551"><strong data-start=3D"455" data-end=
=3D"476">Interoperability:</strong> Enables communication between heterogen=
eous systems (different vendors).</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"552" data-end=3D"634">
<p data-start=3D"555" data-end=3D"634"><strong data-start=3D"555" data-end=
=3D"574">Simplification:</strong> Complex tasks are divided into smaller, m=
anageable units.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"635" data-end=3D"722">
<p data-start=3D"638" data-end=3D"722"><strong data-start=3D"638" data-end=
=3D"658">Troubleshooting:</strong> Easier to identify errors since function=
s are layer-specific.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"723" data-end=3D"826">
<p data-start=3D"726" data-end=3D"826"><strong data-start=3D"726" data-end=
=3D"756">Flexibility &amp; Scalability:</strong> New protocols can be intro=
duced without affecting the entire model.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"827" data-end=3D"914">
<p data-start=3D"830" data-end=3D"914"><strong data-start=3D"830" data-end=
=3D"850">Standardization:</strong> Provides common reference for network de=
sign and communication.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p data-start=3D"916" data-end=3D"967"><strong data-start=3D"916" data-end=
=3D"967">Process-to-Process Delivery in Transport Layer:</strong></p>
<ul data-start=3D"968" data-end=3D"1587">
<li data-start=3D"968" data-end=3D"1107">
<p data-start=3D"970" data-end=3D"1107">The <strong data-start=3D"974" data=
-end=3D"993">Transport Layer</strong> ensures that data is delivered from t=
he <strong data-start=3D"1034" data-end=3D"1053">sending process</strong> (=
application) to the <strong data-start=3D"1075" data-end=3D"1104">correct r=
eceiving process</strong>.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"1108" data-end=3D"1180">
<p data-start=3D"1110" data-end=3D"1180">It uses <strong data-start=3D"1118=
" data-end=3D"1134">port numbers</strong> to uniquely identify processes on=
 a device.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"1181" data-end=3D"1587">
<p data-start=3D"1183" data-end=3D"1206">Provides services like:</p>
<ul data-start=3D"1209" data-end=3D"1587">
<li data-start=3D"1209" data-end=3D"1263">
<p data-start=3D"1211" data-end=3D"1263"><strong data-start=3D"1211" data-e=
nd=3D"1229">Error Control:</strong> Retransmission of lost packets.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"1266" data-end=3D"1331">
<p data-start=3D"1268" data-end=3D"1331"><strong data-start=3D"1268" data-e=
nd=3D"1285">Flow Control:</strong> Prevents sender from overwhelming receiv=
er.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"1334" data-end=3D"1430">
<p data-start=3D"1336" data-end=3D"1430"><strong data-start=3D"1336" data-e=
nd=3D"1366">Segmentation &amp; Reassembly:</strong> Breaks messages into se=
gments and reassembles at destination.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"1433" data-end=3D"1514">
<p data-start=3D"1435" data-end=3D"1514"><strong data-start=3D"1435" data-e=
nd=3D"1474">Connection-Oriented Delivery (TCP):</strong> Reliable, ordered,=
 and error-checked.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"1517" data-end=3D"1587">
<p data-start=3D"1519" data-end=3D"1587"><strong data-start=3D"1519" data-e=
nd=3D"1553">Connectionless Delivery (UDP):</strong> Faster, minimal error-c=
hecking.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start=3D"1589" data-end=3D"1592">
<h2 data-start=3D"1594" data-end=3D"1732"><strong data-start=3D"1597" data-=
end=3D"1732">Q2(a). How does the choice of network architecture impact scal=
ability, reliability, and performance in networking environment? (5M)</stro=
ng></h2>
<h3 data-start=3D"1734" data-end=3D"1749"><strong data-start=3D"1738" data-=
end=3D"1749">Answer:</strong></h3>
<ul data-start=3D"1750" data-end=3D"2285">
<li data-start=3D"1750" data-end=3D"1940">
<p data-start=3D"1752" data-end=3D"1770"><strong data-start=3D"1752" data-e=
nd=3D"1768">Scalability:</strong></p>
<ul data-start=3D"1773" data-end=3D"1940">
<li data-start=3D"1773" data-end=3D"1866">
<p data-start=3D"1775" data-end=3D"1866">Architecture like <strong data-sta=
rt=3D"1793" data-end=3D"1810">Client-Server</strong> can handle many client=
s but may bottleneck at server.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"1869" data-end=3D"1940">
<p data-start=3D"1871" data-end=3D"1940"><strong data-start=3D"1871" data-e=
nd=3D"1893">Peer-to-Peer (P2P)</strong> scales better as each node shares r=
esources.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"1941" data-end=3D"2101">
<p data-start=3D"1943" data-end=3D"1961"><strong data-start=3D"1943" data-e=
nd=3D"1959">Reliability:</strong></p>
<ul data-start=3D"1964" data-end=3D"2101">
<li data-start=3D"1964" data-end=3D"2027">
<p data-start=3D"1966" data-end=3D"2027"><strong data-start=3D"1966" data-e=
nd=3D"1983">Mesh topology</strong> offers high reliability (multiple paths)=
.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"2030" data-end=3D"2101">
<p data-start=3D"2032" data-end=3D"2101"><strong data-start=3D"2032" data-e=
nd=3D"2049">Star topology</strong> is less reliable (hub failure collapses =
network).</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"2102" data-end=3D"2285">
<p data-start=3D"2104" data-end=3D"2122"><strong data-start=3D"2104" data-e=
nd=3D"2120">Performance:</strong></p>
<ul data-start=3D"2125" data-end=3D"2285">
<li data-start=3D"2125" data-end=3D"2208">
<p data-start=3D"2127" data-end=3D"2208"><strong data-start=3D"2127" data-e=
nd=3D"2148">Switched Ethernet</strong> improves performance due to dedicate=
d bandwidth per port.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"2211" data-end=3D"2285">
<p data-start=3D"2213" data-end=3D"2285"><strong data-start=3D"2213" data-e=
nd=3D"2244">Shared media (bus topology)</strong> reduces performance due to=
 collisions.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start=3D"2287" data-end=3D"2290">
<h2 data-start=3D"2292" data-end=3D"2410"><strong data-start=3D"2295" data-=
end=3D"2410">Q2(b). Switch is usually a unicast device but sometimes it bro=
adcasts also. Justify with suitable reasons. (5M)</strong></h2>
<h3 data-start=3D"2412" data-end=3D"2427"><strong data-start=3D"2416" data-=
end=3D"2427">Answer:</strong></h3>
<ul data-start=3D"2428" data-end=3D"2832">
<li data-start=3D"2428" data-end=3D"2548">
<p data-start=3D"2430" data-end=3D"2463"><strong data-start=3D"2430" data-e=
nd=3D"2461">Unicast Function (Default):</strong></p>
<ul data-start=3D"2466" data-end=3D"2548">
<li data-start=3D"2466" data-end=3D"2548">
<p data-start=3D"2468" data-end=3D"2548">Switch forwards frames to a specif=
ic destination <strong data-start=3D"2517" data-end=3D"2532">MAC address</s=
trong> in its table.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"2549" data-end=3D"2832">
<p data-start=3D"2551" data-end=3D"2577"><strong data-start=3D"2551" data-e=
nd=3D"2575">Broadcast Scenarios:</strong></p>
<ol data-start=3D"2580" data-end=3D"2832">
<li data-start=3D"2580" data-end=3D"2683">
<p data-start=3D"2583" data-end=3D"2683"><strong data-start=3D"2583" data-e=
nd=3D"2607">Unknown MAC Address:</strong> If destination MAC not found in s=
witch table =E2=86=92 floods frame to all ports.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"2686" data-end=3D"2764">
<p data-start=3D"2689" data-end=3D"2764"><strong data-start=3D"2689" data-e=
nd=3D"2706">ARP Requests:</strong> For IP-to-MAC resolution, ARP requests a=
re broadcasted.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"2767" data-end=3D"2832">
<p data-start=3D"2770" data-end=3D"2832"><strong data-start=3D"2770" data-e=
nd=3D"2789">DHCP Discovery:</strong> Client broadcasts to find a DHCP serve=
r.</p>
</li>
</ol>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start=3D"2834" data-end=3D"2934">=E2=9C=85 Thus, switch is primaril=
y <strong data-start=3D"2862" data-end=3D"2873">unicast</strong>, but <stro=
ng data-start=3D"2879" data-end=3D"2908">broadcasts when necessary</strong>=
 for discovery/learning.</p>
