<p data-start=3D"3731" data-end=3D"3763"><strong data-start=3D"3731" data-e=
nd=3D"3763">Physical Layer Energy Types:</strong></p>
<ol data-start=3D"3764" data-end=3D"3984">
<li data-start=3D"3764" data-end=3D"3843">
<p data-start=3D"3767" data-end=3D"3843"><strong data-start=3D"3767" data-e=
nd=3D"3791">Electrical Signals =E2=86=92</strong> Carried by copper cables =
(twisted pair, coaxial).</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"3844" data-end=3D"3899">
<p data-start=3D"3847" data-end=3D"3899"><strong data-start=3D"3847" data-e=
nd=3D"3866">Light Signals =E2=86=92</strong> Carried by fiber optic cables.=
</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"3900" data-end=3D"3984">
<p data-start=3D"3903" data-end=3D"3984"><strong data-start=3D"3903" data-e=
nd=3D"3930">Electromagnetic Waves =E2=86=92</strong> Carried in unguided me=
dia (radio, micro, infrared).</p>
</li>
</ol>
<hr data-start=3D"3986" data-end=3D"3989">
<h2 data-start=3D"3991" data-end=3D"4103"><strong data-start=3D"3994" data-=
end=3D"4103">Q4(a). List out all the features, advantages and disadvantages=
 of a point-to-point network topology. (5M)</strong></h2>
<h3 data-start=3D"4105" data-end=3D"4120"><strong data-start=3D"4109" data-=
end=3D"4120">Answer:</strong></h3>
<p data-start=3D"4122" data-end=3D"4135"><strong data-start=3D"4122" data-e=
nd=3D"4135">Features:</strong></p>
<ul data-start=3D"4136" data-end=3D"4222">
<li data-start=3D"4136" data-end=3D"4175">
<p data-start=3D"4138" data-end=3D"4175">Dedicated link between two devices=
.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"4176" data-end=3D"4222">
<p data-start=3D"4178" data-end=3D"4222">Full bandwidth reserved for commun=
ication.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start=3D"4224" data-end=3D"4239"><strong data-start=3D"4224" data-e=
nd=3D"4239">Advantages:</strong></p>
<ol data-start=3D"4240" data-end=3D"4369">
<li data-start=3D"4240" data-end=3D"4286">
<p data-start=3D"4243" data-end=3D"4286">Simple communication between two d=
evices.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"4287" data-end=3D"4322">
<p data-start=3D"4290" data-end=3D"4322">High reliability (no sharing).</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"4323" data-end=3D"4369">
<p data-start=3D"4326" data-end=3D"4369">Better performance due to full ban=
dwidth.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p data-start=3D"4371" data-end=3D"4389"><strong data-start=3D"4371" data-e=
nd=3D"4389">Disadvantages:</strong></p>
<ol data-start=3D"4390" data-end=3D"4545">
<li data-start=3D"4390" data-end=3D"4453">
<p data-start=3D"4393" data-end=3D"4453">Not scalable (requires many connec=
tions for more devices).</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"4454" data-end=3D"4505">
<p data-start=3D"4457" data-end=3D"4505">Expensive for large networks (n(n=
=E2=80=931)/2 links).</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"4506" data-end=3D"4545">
<p data-start=3D"4509" data-end=3D"4545">Redundancy and maintenance issues.=
</p>
</li>
</ol>
<hr data-start=3D"4547" data-end=3D"4550">
<h2 data-start=3D"4552" data-end=3D"4662"><strong data-start=3D"4555" data-=
end=3D"4662">Q4(b). Consider a small office with five computers connected=
=E2=80=A6 Which architecture is most suitable? (5M)</strong></h2>
<h3 data-start=3D"4664" data-end=3D"4679"><strong data-start=3D"4668" data-=
end=3D"4679">Answer:</strong></h3>
<ul data-start=3D"4680" data-end=3D"4986">
<li data-start=3D"4680" data-end=3D"4760">
<p data-start=3D"4682" data-end=3D"4760"><strong data-start=3D"4682" data-e=
nd=3D"4695">Scenario:</strong> Each of the 5 computers must directly commun=
icate with others.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"4761" data-end=3D"4986">
<p data-start=3D"4763" data-end=3D"4775"><strong data-start=3D"4763" data-e=
nd=3D"4775">Options:</strong></p>
<ul data-start=3D"4778" data-end=3D"4986">
<li data-start=3D"4778" data-end=3D"4854">
<p data-start=3D"4780" data-end=3D"4854"><strong data-start=3D"4780" data-e=
nd=3D"4798">Mesh Topology:</strong> Provides direct links but costly (10 li=
nks required).</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"4857" data-end=3D"4986">
<p data-start=3D"4859" data-end=3D"4986"><strong data-start=3D"4859" data-e=
nd=3D"4889">Star Topology with Switch:</strong> Central device (switch) con=
nects all 5 computers. Each can communicate with others via switch.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start=3D"4988" data-end=3D"5046"><strong data-start=3D"4988" data-e=
nd=3D"5006">Most Suitable:</strong><br data-start=3D"5006" data-end=3D"5009=
">
=F0=9F=91=89 <strong data-start=3D"5012" data-end=3D"5044">Star Topology us=
ing a Switch</strong></p>
<p data-start=3D"5048" data-end=3D"5066"><strong data-start=3D"5048" data-e=
nd=3D"5066">Justification:</strong></p>
<ul data-start=3D"5067" data-end=3D"5258">
<li data-start=3D"5067" data-end=3D"5113">
<p data-start=3D"5069" data-end=3D"5113">Scalable and easy to add/remove co=
mputers.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"5114" data-end=3D"5141">
<p data-start=3D"5116" data-end=3D"5141">Cheaper than full mesh.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"5142" data-end=3D"5206">
<p data-start=3D"5144" data-end=3D"5206">Reliable (if one cable fails, only=
 that device is affected).</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"5207" data-end=3D"5258">
<p data-start=3D"5209" data-end=3D"5258">Provides high performance with ded=
icated links.</p>
</li>
</ul>
