iv class=3D"markdown prose dark:prose-invert w-full break-words dark markdo=
wn-new-styling"><p data-start=3D"0" data-end=3D"202">Great question! =F0=9F=
=91=8D<br data-start=3D"18" data-end=3D"21">
This diagram represents <strong data-start=3D"45" data-end=3D"84">the OSI M=
odel encapsulation process</strong> =E2=80=93 how data is prepared and tran=
smitted from the sender to the receiver across a network. Let me explain st=
ep by step:</p>
<hr data-start=3D"204" data-end=3D"207">
<h3 data-start=3D"209" data-end=3D"245"><strong data-start=3D"213" data-end=
=3D"245">1. OSI Layers in the Diagram</strong></h3>
<p data-start=3D"246" data-end=3D"284">The figure shows all <strong data-st=
art=3D"267" data-end=3D"283">7 OSI layers</strong>:</p>
<ol data-start=3D"285" data-end=3D"813">
<li data-start=3D"285" data-end=3D"360">
<p data-start=3D"288" data-end=3D"360"><strong data-start=3D"288" data-end=
=3D"309">Application Layer</strong> =E2=80=93 End-user interaction (e.g., w=
eb browser, email).</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"361" data-end=3D"430">
<p data-start=3D"364" data-end=3D"430"><strong data-start=3D"364" data-end=
=3D"386">Presentation Layer</strong> =E2=80=93 Data formatting, encryption,=
 compression.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"431" data-end=3D"508">
<p data-start=3D"434" data-end=3D"508"><strong data-start=3D"434" data-end=
=3D"451">Session Layer</strong> =E2=80=93 Establishes, manages, and ends co=
mmunication sessions.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"509" data-end=3D"585">
<p data-start=3D"512" data-end=3D"585"><strong data-start=3D"512" data-end=
=3D"531">Transport Layer</strong> =E2=80=93 Provides reliable or unreliable=
 delivery (TCP/UDP).</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"586" data-end=3D"653">
<p data-start=3D"589" data-end=3D"653"><strong data-start=3D"589" data-end=
=3D"606">Network Layer</strong> =E2=80=93 Logical addressing &amp; routing =
(IP addresses).</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"654" data-end=3D"723">
<p data-start=3D"657" data-end=3D"723"><strong data-start=3D"657" data-end=
=3D"676">Data Link Layer</strong> =E2=80=93 Physical addressing (MAC) + err=
or detection.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"724" data-end=3D"813">
<p data-start=3D"727" data-end=3D"813"><strong data-start=3D"727" data-end=
=3D"745">Physical Layer</strong> =E2=80=93 Transmission over physical mediu=
m (cables, signals, radio waves).</p>
</li>
</ol>
<hr data-start=3D"815" data-end=3D"818">
<h3 data-start=3D"820" data-end=3D"868"><strong data-start=3D"824" data-end=
=3D"868">2. What the Picture Shows: Encapsulation</strong></h3>
<p data-start=3D"869" data-end=3D"983">When data is passed <strong data-sta=
rt=3D"889" data-end=3D"908">down the layers</strong>, each layer <strong da=
ta-start=3D"921" data-end=3D"944">adds its own header</strong> (and sometim=
es trailer) to the data.</p>
<p data-start=3D"985" data-end=3D"1102">=F0=9F=94=B9 This process is called=
 <strong data-start=3D"1011" data-end=3D"1028">encapsulation</strong>.<br d=
ata-start=3D"1029" data-end=3D"1032">
=F0=9F=94=B9 On the receiving side, the process is reversed (<strong data-s=
tart=3D"1083" data-end=3D"1100">decapsulation</strong>).</p>
<hr data-start=3D"1104" data-end=3D"1107">
<h3 data-start=3D"1109" data-end=3D"1136"><strong data-start=3D"1113" data-=
end=3D"1136">3. Flow Explanation</strong></h3>
<ul data-start=3D"1137" data-end=3D"1794">
<li data-start=3D"1137" data-end=3D"1208">
<p data-start=3D"1139" data-end=3D"1208">At the <strong data-start=3D"1146"=
 data-end=3D"1167">Application Layer</strong>, user data is created (e.g., =
a message).</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"1209" data-end=3D"1277">
<p data-start=3D"1211" data-end=3D"1277">The <strong data-start=3D"1215" da=
ta-end=3D"1237">Application Header</strong> is added =E2=86=92 (Application=
 Header + Data).</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"1278" data-end=3D"1346">
<p data-start=3D"1280" data-end=3D"1346">At the <strong data-start=3D"1287"=
 data-end=3D"1309">Presentation Layer</strong>, a <strong data-start=3D"131=
3" data-end=3D"1336">Presentation Header</strong> is added.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"1347" data-end=3D"1405">
<p data-start=3D"1349" data-end=3D"1405">At the <strong data-start=3D"1356"=
 data-end=3D"1373">Session Layer</strong>, a <strong data-start=3D"1377" da=
ta-end=3D"1395">Session Header</strong> is added.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"1406" data-end=3D"1489">
<p data-start=3D"1408" data-end=3D"1489">At the <strong data-start=3D"1415"=
 data-end=3D"1434">Transport Layer</strong>, a <strong data-start=3D"1438" =
data-end=3D"1458">Transport Header</strong> (e.g., TCP/UDP info) is added.<=
/p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"1490" data-end=3D"1563">
<p data-start=3D"1492" data-end=3D"1563">At the <strong data-start=3D"1499"=
 data-end=3D"1516">Network Layer</strong>, a <strong data-start=3D"1520" da=
ta-end=3D"1538">Network Header</strong> (IP addresses) is added.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"1564" data-end=3D"1693">
<p data-start=3D"1566" data-end=3D"1693">At the <strong data-start=3D"1573"=
 data-end=3D"1592">Data Link Layer</strong>, a <strong data-start=3D"1596" =
data-end=3D"1616">Data Link Header</strong> (MAC addresses) and <strong dat=
a-start=3D"1637" data-end=3D"1648">Trailer</strong> (error check) are added=
 =E2=86=92 forms a <strong data-start=3D"1683" data-end=3D"1692">frame</str=
ong>.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"1694" data-end=3D"1794">
<p data-start=3D"1696" data-end=3D"1794">At the <strong data-start=3D"1703"=
 data-end=3D"1721">Physical Layer</strong>, the complete frame is converted=
 into <strong data-start=3D"1760" data-end=3D"1776">bits/signals</strong> f=
or transmission.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start=3D"1796" data-end=3D"1799">
<h3 data-start=3D"1801" data-end=3D"1831"><strong data-start=3D"1805" data-=
end=3D"1831">4. Encapsulation Units</strong></h3>
<ul data-start=3D"1832" data-end=3D"2036">
<li data-start=3D"1832" data-end=3D"1886">
<p data-start=3D"1834" data-end=3D"1886"><strong data-start=3D"1834" data-e=
nd=3D"1877">Application/Presentation/Session Layers</strong> =E2=86=92 Data=
</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"1887" data-end=3D"1943">
<p data-start=3D"1889" data-end=3D"1943"><strong data-start=3D"1889" data-e=
nd=3D"1908">Transport Layer</strong> =E2=86=92 Segment (TCP) / Datagram (UD=
P)</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"1944" data-end=3D"1974">
<p data-start=3D"1946" data-end=3D"1974"><strong data-start=3D"1946" data-e=
nd=3D"1963">Network Layer</strong> =E2=86=92 Packet</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"1975" data-end=3D"2006">
<p data-start=3D"1977" data-end=3D"2006"><strong data-start=3D"1977" data-e=
nd=3D"1996">Data Link Layer</strong> =E2=86=92 Frame</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"2007" data-end=3D"2036">
<p data-start=3D"2009" data-end=3D"2036"><strong data-start=3D"2009" data-e=
nd=3D"2027">Physical Layer</strong> =E2=86=92 Bits</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start=3D"2038" data-end=3D"2041">
<h3 data-start=3D"2043" data-end=3D"2066"><strong data-start=3D"2047" data-=
end=3D"2066">5. Key Takeaway</strong></h3>
<p data-start=3D"2067" data-end=3D"2088">The picture explains:</p>
<ul data-start=3D"2089" data-end=3D"2344">
<li data-start=3D"2089" data-end=3D"2168">
<p data-start=3D"2091" data-end=3D"2168">How each OSI layer adds its own <s=
trong data-start=3D"2123" data-end=3D"2146">control information</strong> (h=
eaders/trailers).</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"2169" data-end=3D"2256">
<p data-start=3D"2171" data-end=3D"2256">The headers contain necessary deta=
ils (like addressing, sequencing, error control).</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"2257" data-end=3D"2344">
<p data-start=3D"2259" data-end=3D"2344">Data becomes progressively larger =
as it goes down (because more headers are added).</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start=3D"2346" data-end=3D"2503">=F0=9F=91=89 At the receiver side,=
 the reverse happens: <strong data-start=3D"2392" data-end=3D"2439">each la=
yer removes its corresponding header</strong> and processes the data until =
the original message is delivered.</p>
<hr data-start=3D"2505" data-end=3D"2508">
<p data-start=3D"2510" data-end=3D"2662">=E2=9A=A1 In short: This image is =
showing <strong data-start=3D"2544" data-end=3D"2662">how data travels thro=
ugh the OSI layers with headers added at each stage (encapsulation) before =
being transmitted.</strong></p>
